# C#/.NET patterns

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic design guidance to
idiomatic C#/.NET design and its common anti-patterns: async/await,
dependency injection, LINQ, and disposal. Applies only to `*.cs` files.

## Async/await

- Never use `async void` except for an actual event handler
  (`private async void Button_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)`) — any
  exception an `async void` method throws cannot be awaited or caught by
  its caller and crashes the process instead. Every other async method
  returns `Task` or `Task<T>`.
- Return `Task`/`Task<T>` for a method whose async work is on the common,
  usually-awaited path; return `ValueTask`/`ValueTask<T>` only for a
  hot-path method that frequently completes synchronously (a cache hit),
  where avoiding the `Task` allocation is measured to matter — a
  `ValueTask` must be awaited (or converted) exactly once and never
  stored, unlike a `Task`.
- In library code that does not need to resume on a captured context
  (UI thread, ASP.NET classic `SynchronizationContext`), call
  `.ConfigureAwait(false)` on an awaited `Task` to avoid an unnecessary
  context-capture and reduce deadlock risk. Modern ASP.NET Core has no
  such `SynchronizationContext`, so application-level request-handling
  code in an ASP.NET Core project does not need it — match what the
  project's own code already does.
- Never call `.Result`, `.Wait()`, or `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` on a
  `Task` from otherwise-synchronous code to "call an async method
  synchronously" — this is sync-over-async and can deadlock when the
  awaited call needs to resume on a context the blocking thread is
  itself occupying. Make the calling method `async` and `await` instead;
  if the call truly cannot become async (a legacy synchronous interface
  boundary), that is a design problem to flag, not to paper over.

## Dependency injection

- Register a service at the narrowest lifetime that is correct:
  `Singleton` for stateless or thread-safe shared state, `Scoped` for
  per-request/per-unit-of-work state (a `DbContext`, a request-scoped
  cache), `Transient` for cheap, stateless, or intentionally
  non-shared instances. Never inject a `Scoped` service into a
  `Singleton` by capturing it directly — that captures the first
  request's instance for the app's lifetime; resolve it per-use via
  `IServiceScopeFactory`/`IServiceProvider.CreateScope()` instead.
- Depend on an interface (`IOrderRepository`), not a concrete
  implementation, at a constructor boundary that DI resolves — this is
  what makes the type unit-testable without a real database/HTTP call.
- Prefer constructor injection for required dependencies; reserve
  property injection for genuinely optional dependencies with a working
  default, which is uncommon.

## LINQ efficiency

- Materialize a query with `.ToList()`/`.ToArray()` before enumerating it
  more than once (in a loop that also iterates it, or across a
  `.Count()` followed by a `foreach`) — an `IEnumerable<T>` built from a
  `IQueryable<T>`/deferred LINQ chain re-runs the whole query (or, worse,
  a whole database round trip) on every enumeration unless it is
  materialized once.
- Avoid stacking multiple `.Where()`/`.Select()` passes over the same
  large sequence when one combined pass would do — each pass is a full
  additional enumeration for an in-memory `IEnumerable<T>`.
- On an `IQueryable<T>` backed by a database (EF Core), keep filtering
  (`.Where`) and projection (`.Select`) in the query itself so they
  translate to SQL, rather than calling `.ToList()` early and then
  filtering in memory — the latter pulls the whole table across the wire
  first.
- Use `.Any()` to check for existence, never `.Count() > 0`, which forces
  a full count when a short-circuiting existence check would do.

## Disposal patterns

- Any local or field holding an `IDisposable` is wrapped in a `using`
  statement/declaration (`using var conn = new SqlConnection(...);`) or
  disposed in a `Dispose()`/`finally` block — never left for the
  finalizer, which is non-deterministic and, for something like an open
  connection or file handle, holds the resource far longer than needed.
- Use `await using` for an `IAsyncDisposable` (most modern EF Core/
  streaming/channel types expose one) so disposal itself is awaited
  instead of blocking a thread on synchronous `Dispose()`.
- A class that owns one or more `IDisposable`/`IAsyncDisposable`
  fields implements `IDisposable`/`IAsyncDisposable` itself and disposes
  them in its own `Dispose`/`DisposeAsync`, rather than requiring every
  caller to know which of its fields need cleanup.

## Anti-patterns to flag

- A "god service"/"utils" static class that accumulates unrelated
  helper methods instead of being split along what the code actually
  does — the same smell as a Go "god package," expressed as a static
  class here.
- Catching `Exception` broadly and continuing as if nothing happened,
  instead of catching the specific exception type a call can throw (or
  letting an unexpected one propagate) — see `rules/security.mdc` and
  `dotnet-code-review`'s focus list for the review-time version of this
  check.
- Exposing a mutable `List<T>`/collection field directly as a public
  property instead of `IReadOnlyList<T>`/`IReadOnlyCollection<T>` (or a
  defensive copy), which lets any caller mutate internal state through a
  reference they were only meant to read.
